<!-- Toy picker preview in the system's Glyph Matrix icon style: a black circle
     carrying the LED grid, unlit cells as faint texture, and a key lit on top.

     THIS USED TO SPELL "GM"/"TC" in the 3x5 pixel font — the sort of stale
     branding a text search never finds, because the letters were drawn as vector
     rectangles. It is also the tile Nothing OS shows in the Glyph Toy picker, so
     it was the most visible piece of the old name anywhere in the product.

     ## Why a key, and not the app's dot logo

     The tile's job is not to repeat the launcher icon. It sits in a picker full
     of other toys, and the question it has to answer is "which of these is
     which". The dot mark answered that badly: a 3x3 grid of dots drawn on a grid
     of dots reads as texture rather than as a symbol at 48 dp, and it says
     nothing about what selecting it does.

     A key does. The Essential Key is the whole reason this app exists, it is the
     one thing it has that no other toy in that list has, and the silhouette
     survives being drawn in 27 cells.

     ## It is the app's own art, not a new drawing

     Lifted from ART_KEY in ui/OnboardingActivity.kt — the shape the intro morphs
     the logo into — with the shaft shortened from 17 columns to 13. Same 7x7 bow,
     same centre dot, same two teeth. The picker tile and the first screen of
     onboarding now show the user the same key, which is the point.

     ## The geometry is constrained by the disc, not by taste

     Grid unchanged from the old file so nothing else shifts: 13x13, 3.0 pitch,
     2.6 squares, clipped to the circle. That clip is the constraint — the corner
     cells of a 13x13 square fall OUTSIDE a circle inscribed in it, so the unlit
     path below is short on every row except the middle nine.

     The key takes rows 3..9 and columns 0..12: seven rows, centred, with the
     shaft on row 6 so it runs along the disc's widest line. That is what lets it
     reach both edges — columns 0 and 12 exist only on those middle rows, and a
     lit cell with no unlit cell under it renders as a white square on the
     transparent background instead of on the disc. An earlier draft of this file
     put three of them there. Check any edit with the script in the scratchpad
     that rasterises the result and counts strays.

     Only two fills now: the mark is one flat white. The dot logo needed a third
     for the dimmed dots it inherited from the launcher icon; a key has nothing
     to dim. -->
